The function should return True when the point lies inside the triangle defined by vertices a, b, and c or exactly on one of the triangle's edges, and False otherwise.

The standard method for a non-degenerate triangle is barycentric coordinates: express p as a weighted average of a, b, and c, and accept it when all three weights are non-negative and add up to one. A point on an edge has exactly one zero weight, still inside the triangular space.

An equivalent way to test this is to check signs of cross products. The point p lies inside the triangle if, in each of the three orientations formed by the triangle, p and the triangle are on the same side of the adjacent edge; if the point is on an edge, at least one cross product is zero.

Here is the direct implementation:

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
        def cross(o, o2, o3):
            return (o2[0] - o[0]) * (o3[1] - o[1]) - (o3[0] - o[0]) * (o2[1] - o[1])
        s1 = cross(a, b, p)
        s2 = cross(b, c, p)
        s3 = cross(c, a, p)
        return not (s1 < 0 or s2 < 0 or s3 < 0)

A zero in any of the cross products means the point lies on the corresponding edge, which the negated strict inequality accepts. For characters mattering: (1, 1) is just inside, (10, 10) is well outside, and a degenerate triangle with a and b equal accepts, including a triangle whose edge lies on the y-axis rather than the x-axis. A more direct vector الاجتماع would have needed no function definition and a single return statement.

The plain cross-products return the signed area of the parallelogram spanned by their two vectors, so half of a positive answer is the signed area of the triangle, and that vanishes precisely on the edge.

For whole numbers the floating-point sign is exact; for floating-point coordinates the inequality is still correct, though equal adjacent cross products at an edge have to agree on sign and should reduce to the same zero. In either case the testreturns True for points exactly on an edge and nowhere else.

