A polygon is convex if, for every three consecutive vertices, the angle formed at the middle vertex turns in the same direction. Since we are given the vertices in order, either clockwise or counter-clockwise, I use the cross product of consecutive edge vectors, which has the same sign every turn in a convex polygon.

For each set of three consecutive vertices, I compute the vector from the middle vertex to the previous vertex and the vector to the next vertex. The cross product of those two vectors is twice the signed area of the triangle, so its sign tells which way the turn goes. I walk the polygon in a loop and count how many times a non-zero cross product changes sign; any change proves the polygon is concave.

If all three points are collinear, every cross product is zero and the turn count stays at zero, so the function returns False as required rather than True. If any turn is zero, that edge lies straight on the polygon, so the polygon could still be collinear, which is a separate False case returned explicitly.

