The point p lies inside the triangle or on one of its edges when it lies on the same side of each of the triangle's edges as the third vertex. Take edge bc and the third vertex a. Let v be the vector from b to c and w the vector from b to p; p is on the same side as a if the cross product v × w and v × a have the same sign. A zero product means p lies on the line. Repeating the test for edges ca and ab with their respective third vertices likewise gives zero when p lies on an edge, and unless all three signs are the same the point lies outside.

