The Python code follows the straightforward approach of building all four rotations of the first grid and comparing each with the second:

    def equal_under_rotation(grid1, grid2):
        from itertools import chain
        if not grid1:
            return not grid2 or not any(grid2)
        if grid1 and not grid2:
            return False
        n = len(grid1)
        rotate = [[grid1[n - j - 1][i] for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]
        rotate180 = [[grid1[n - i - 1][n - j - 1] for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]
        rotate270 = [[grid1[j][n - i - 1] for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]
        for g in (grid1, rotate, rotate180, rotate270):
            for i in range(n):
                if tuple(g[i]) != grid2[i]:
                    break
            else:
                return True
        return False

The functions use list comprehensions that choose the characters in each row following each of the four rotation directions, transposing the grid as needed between 90 and 270 degrees and reversing rows and columns as needed in the other rotations. The loop tests equality row by row for each of the four derived grids.

