The function must return a flood fill result on a grid: the cell at (sr, sc) and every cell reachable from it through horizontal and vertical neighbours having the same original value is set to new_value, and the caller's grid must not be altered. If new_value equals the value at (sr, sc), an unchanged copy is returned.

My implementation first copies the grid, so the caller's grid is never modified, then uses a stack-based depth-first traversal:

    def flood_fill(grid, sr, sc, new_value):
        from copy import deepcopy
        if grid[sr][sc] == new_value:
            return deepcopy(grid)
        value = grid[sr][sc]
        result = deepcopy(grid)
        stack = [(sr, sc)]
        while stack:
            r, c = stack.pop()
            if result[r][c] == value:
                result[r][c] = new_value
                if r > 0:
                    stack.append((r - 1, c))
                if r + 1 < len(grid):
                    stack.append((r + 1, c))
                if c > 0:
                    stack.append((r, c - 1))
                if c + 1 < len(grid[0]):
                    stack.append((r, c + 1))
        return result

The incoming grid is deep-copied so any write can happen safely without side effects. The value at (sr, sc) is stored under its original name, and the first row of the copy is pushed onto a stack as the starting point. The body replaces an entry when it still holds the original value, to prevent reprocessing cells, and appends the cell's four accessible neighbours. Each of these is popped, checked and filled in turn, which is how a deep copy speeds things up, as unvisited cells never change. The early return skips the whole scan when the new value already matches the original, as the specification asks.Each fallback pushes a neighbouring point after having overwritten the original cell, so those cells are not processed before the value has changed and they are marked visited by the time they come off the stack.

