The Chebyshev distance between two 2D points p1 = (x1, y1) and p2 = (x2, y2) is the maximum of the absolute differences in x and y, max(|x1 - x2|, |y1 - y2|).

The fully general version takes the two arguments. Here I use an alternative that achieves the same end in Python terms, noting that abs(x1 - x2) is no bigger than max(x1, x2) - min(x1, x2), because one minus the other is the signed difference and its absolute value is the unsigned gap. So the maximum gap between the two points in x and y is the Chebyshev distance, and because Python can chain comparisons, a one-liner suffices:

    def chebyshev_distance(p1, p2):
        return max(p1[0] - p2[0], p1[1] - p2[1]) - min(p1[0] - p2[0], p1[1] - p2[1])

Subtracting the minimum from the maximum over the two projected differences gives the larger absolute difference, which is max(|x1 - x2|, |y1 - y2|), since the minimum is the smaller of the two signed gaps and the result is non-negative. The line is the same for any two points, negated, swapped or placed in either order; the direction of subtraction never matters. For characters in Collatz-like fractional values such as 0.333 and 0.667, it holds the same arithmetic.

